R["«EXEC»"] = """*(Synthetic customers with a hidden counterfactual, built on the documented design of Zomato Gold (₹149 for three months, free delivery, over nine lakh members within a month) and Flipkart Plus tiers. **The evidence is weak:** no company measurement method is public, so this is an interview-standard solution on simulated data, not a reproduction of any company's system. Written after the analysis.)*

- **The question:** did the membership really raise order frequency, and does it pay once free delivery is counted? Members chose to join, so the naive comparison is not a causal answer.
- **The naive gap is mostly selection.** After launch, members order **3.78** times a month against **2.22** for never-members (a gap of **+1.55, +70%**). Before the programme existed the same gap was already **+0.65 (+26%)**. The hidden true effect is **+0.395 orders a month (+11.7%)**.
- **Matching and difference-in-differences did not fix it here.** Matching on levels gave +1.10 (CI 0.92 to 1.27); adding trend covariates balanced everything observed (max |SMD| 0.04) and still gave **+0.92 (0.73 to 1.10)**. Difference-in-differences gave **+0.91 to +1.02**. Every interval misses the truth, and the pre-launch placebo tests passed (p = 0.35 to 0.85), so **passing a parallel-trend check did not make the estimate right**.
- **The randomised free-trial pilot recovered the truth, but at a price in precision.** The invitation raised joining from 12.8% to 40.6% (F = 3,437). The instrumented effect is **+0.33 orders a month (CI 0.24 to 0.43)**, which covers the truth, but the interval is **0.6 times as wide as the estimate**.
- **Frequency is not margin.** Matched GOV rose **₹521 a member-month**, yet the matched margin estimate was **+₹1.8 (CI −4.3 to +7.8)** against a hidden truth of **−₹37.7**. Observational methods got the sign of the profit question wrong. Retention looked **+13.2 points** matched against a true **+2.7**.
- **Who gains:** by the matched estimates, low-frequency customers gain margin (+₹43) and high-frequency customers lose it (−₹39). The levels are biased upward, but the ordering is plausible, because heavy users take the subsidy on orders they would have placed anyway.
- **Money (assumed, 9 lakh members):** the matched estimate implies **about ₹1.9 crore a year (−4.6 to +8.4)**; a naive reading would have claimed **₹97 crore**; the hidden truth is about **−₹41 crore**. Treat the programme's margin as at risk until a holdout says otherwise.
- **A/B holdout:** a random holdout needs about **911 customers per arm** to detect a ₹10 margin change a month, or **691 per arm** for +0.2 orders a month.
- **I recommend:** keep a persistent random holdout, judge the programme on contribution margin, do not publish an uplift from member-vs-non-member comparisons, and test a higher fee or targeted enrolment before expanding."""
