R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation**

1. **Do not report an uplift from member-versus-non-member comparisons.** They show +70% orders against a true +12%. Stop quoting "frequency up X% after joining".
2. **Build a persistent random holdout into the programme**, a few thousand customers (about 900 per arm detects a ₹10 margin change a month; more for subgroups). It is the only design that recovered the truth here.
3. **Judge the programme on contribution margin per customer-month**, not GOV, frequency or retention. Every observational method looked fine on those and was wrong on margin.
4. **Do not expand until a holdout shows margin is not negative.** Meanwhile test a **higher fee or a free-delivery limit** (for example a minimum basket or distance), and a **lighter benefit for the heaviest orderers**, who lose the most.
5. **Use matching and difference-in-differences only as a cross-check**, with a pre window that excludes the months that led to joining, and with the result reported next to the pilot estimate.

**Decision rule:** expand only if the holdout shows a contribution effect of at least zero per member-month with the lower end of the 95% interval above about −₹10, and a frequency effect above +0.1 orders a month. If the margin interval is clearly negative, re-price or restrict benefits before growing.

**Not yet known:** behaviour at renewal prices, effect on non-members and restaurant partners, long-run retention, and whether earned tiers (Flipkart Plus style) behave differently, which a threshold analysis could test. All numbers come from simulated data."""
